1
00:00:01,420 --> 00:00:03,640
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:04,300
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:08,600
My name is Kathryn and we have an unusual lesson for you today.

4
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:10,900
It's more about writing than about speaking.

5
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:11,960
That's right.

6
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:20,680
Well, many times we have to write emails and if we're working in a sales environment, we may need to write an email to a potential client.

7
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:25,020
Alright, so we're going to learn what is appropriate and what is not in today's lesson.

8
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:26,800
Let's take a listen to our dialogue.

9
00:00:34,190 --> 00:00:35,990
Melanie, can you help me with something?

10
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:43,030
We need to finalize the account with the Mexican embassy and I need some advice on phrasing this letter correctly in English.

11
00:00:43,810 --> 00:00:45,470
Sure Tracy, let me just get my laptop.

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,760
Right, all set.

13
00:00:56,760 --> 00:01:01,840
Ok, so, to whom it may concern, I am writing…

14
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:05,760
Um, Tracy, I think that's a little too formal.

15
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:12,390
I know you want to be polite, but you've already made contact with them, so in English, you can be a bit more relaxed in the opening.

16
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:14,720
Ok, more relaxed.

17
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:15,720
Got it.

18
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:16,870
Hey Sally, what's up?

19
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:18,990
It's Tracy here, just…

20
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:23,160
Ok, Tracy, now it's too relaxed.

21
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:24,390
You've still got to show some respect.

22
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:28,250
How about starting with Dear Miss Cooper, I'm writing to confirm…

23
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:30,760
Great, ok.

24
00:01:30,810 --> 00:01:46,540
Dear Miss Cooper, I'm writing to confirm the final quotation for the full-page back cover color advertisement you requested for the spring issue of Viola Magazine.

25
00:01:47,850 --> 00:01:48,570
That's great.

26
00:01:49,720 --> 00:02:00,560
The final costing, including advert design and production, comes to 45,600 RMB.

27
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:07,310
We want payment 10 working days before publication, or we will cancel the ad.

28
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:09,480
Thanks for…

29
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:12,320
Whoa, ok, back up a second Tracy.

30
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:13,510
That's too direct.

31
00:02:14,310 --> 00:02:20,130
Can I suggest you say, please note that the final payment is due two working weeks before publication.

32
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:22,280
You don't want to offend her.

33
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:24,920
Oops, ok, you're right.

34
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:27,460
Then I can just end with, all the best, Tracy.

35
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:33,680
Hmm, maybe I'd play it safe and just finish with, yours sincerely.

36
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:34,300
That's more professional.

37
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:36,920
Oh, Melanie, you're a lifesaver.

38
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:37,740
Thank you.

39
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:51,200
Alright, we're back, so now let's take a look at some of that vocab we heard on Language Takeaway.

40
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:51,810
Language Takeaway.

41
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:55,820
We have some wonderful adjectives today.

42
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:59,320
The first one that I wanted to discuss, Marco, is formal.

43
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:00,680
Formal, that's right.

44
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:05,020
We want to be a little bit more formal when we're writing someone we don't really know too much.

45
00:03:05,220 --> 00:03:05,660
That's right.

46
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:09,220
But be careful because sometimes you can be too formal.

47
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:11,480
So what exactly does formal mean?

48
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:16,360
So if you write a formal email, it's very proper, very respectful.

49
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:25,000
So proper in the way that you wouldn't use colloquial terms or phrasal verbs that you would use with your friends.

50
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
No, a good example is writing to a lawyer or a judge.

51
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:32,000
You want to use formal language.

52
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:34,960
Even with a teacher, you might use more formal language.

53
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:37,360
But with a friend, you don't have to.

54
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:38,140
That's something else.

55
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:38,840
That's right.

56
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:43,310
So when you're writing something that's formal, it's important to be polite as well.

57
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:44,600
Now what is polite?

58
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:44,790
Right.

59
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:48,960
So you want to be formal, you want to be serious, and you want to be polite.

60
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:49,850
You want to show respect.

61
00:03:50,630 --> 00:03:52,390
You want to be nice, basically.

62
00:03:52,930 --> 00:03:53,350
That's right.

63
00:03:54,220 --> 00:03:56,680
And so people can often be described as polite.

64
00:03:56,680 --> 00:03:58,850
He's very polite or she's not polite at all.

65
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:03,520
And when I was a little kid, my mom used to say, it's very important to be polite.

66
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:07,160
You say thank you and you're welcome when you're talking to other people.

67
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:07,290
That's right.

68
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:09,250
And that's one way of being polite.

69
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:16,950
Now also when we are describing the way that you speak to someone or the way that you write, you can be very direct.

70
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:21,350
Now being direct means that you say exactly what you want or exactly what you're thinking.

71
00:04:22,030 --> 00:04:26,690
Okay, so maybe if we're talking to this company, you say, hi, I want this.

72
00:04:27,110 --> 00:04:27,870
I will pay this much.

73
00:04:28,170 --> 00:04:28,490
Thank you.

74
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:30,360
Bye.

75
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:31,360
Right.

76
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:32,360
That's maybe too direct.

77
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:36,180
You need to be a little less direct and say nice things in an email.

78
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:37,800
That's right.

79
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:42,800
So maybe you want to say to someone that you don't really like the clothes they're wearing, right?

80
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:44,600
You say, hey, do you like my new shirt?

81
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:47,700
And if you're very direct, you would say, no, I think it's very ugly.

82
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:48,700
I hate it.

83
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:49,240
Right.

84
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:50,180
That's too direct.

85
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:55,040
So maybe you would say, I don't really think it suits you very well.

86
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:57,850
Or I think a different color would be better on you.

87
00:04:58,200 --> 00:04:58,370
That's right.

88
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:00,450
So that's being a little bit less direct.

89
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:06,130
Now obviously, if you're too direct with someone, you may offend that person.

90
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:15,500
Alright, to offend is a verb and to offend someone means to say something that makes them unhappy or upset or even angry.

91
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:21,390
So going back to the same example of the shirt, if you say, I think that shirt is very ugly, that person might be offended.

92
00:05:22,130 --> 00:05:22,610
Gosh.

93
00:05:22,870 --> 00:05:31,460
Or maybe if you say, I think people with brown hair are so ugly and there's someone next to me with brown hair, they say, oh, I'm offended.

94
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:32,160
Right.

95
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:34,240
I'm not ugly, but I have brown hair.

96
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:34,860
That's right.

97
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:42,180
Okay, so you don't want to offend anyone by being too direct or you don't want to have anyone feel offended.

98
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:53,690
Now for our last phrase here on Language Takeaway, we have towards the end of the dialogue, Tracy thanks Melanie by saying, you are a lifesaver.

99
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:54,430
Thank you.

100
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:56,230
Okay, so don't be confused.

101
00:05:56,680 --> 00:06:00,410
There is also a candy called lifesavers and she's not calling her a candy.

102
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:02,510
She's saying, wow, thank you.

103
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:03,650
You really helped me.

104
00:06:04,410 --> 00:06:07,530
So when someone helps you a lot, you can call them a lifesaver.

105
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:11,880
Marco, what exactly is the object, the lifesaver?

106
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:22,480
If you've ever been on a boat, you see this big orange circle that is used to throw into the water to help someone to prevent them from drowning.

107
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:24,080
That is called a lifesaver.

108
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:25,920
Okay, so I'm drowning.

109
00:06:25,970 --> 00:06:27,590
I'm having a hard time writing this email.

110
00:06:27,890 --> 00:06:29,450
I do not know what to say.

111
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:31,030
Marco, you come and help me.

112
00:06:31,230 --> 00:06:33,550
I can say, oh wow, thank you so much.

113
00:06:33,570 --> 00:06:34,850
You're a lifesaver.

114
00:06:35,090 --> 00:06:36,150
That's exactly right.

115
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:39,270
All right, so we've taken a look at five key words there.

116
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:43,710
Why don't we take a listen to our dialogue again and we'll be back with Fluency Builder.

