120
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:13,240
Right, all set.

121
00:07:13,950 --> 00:07:18,500
Okay, so to whom it may concern, I am writing...

122
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,680
Tracy, I think that's a little too formal.

123
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:29,160
I know you want to be polite, but you've already made contact with them, so in English, you can be a bit more relaxed in the opening.

124
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:30,580
Okay, more relaxed.

125
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:32,520
Got it.

126
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:33,650
Hey Sally, what's up?

127
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:35,410
It's Tracy here, just...

128
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:38,970
Okay Tracy, now it's too relaxed.

129
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:41,210
You've still got to show some respect.

130
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:45,050
How about starting with, dear Miss Cooper, I'm writing to confirm...

131
00:07:46,010 --> 00:07:46,630
Great, okay.

132
00:07:47,260 --> 00:08:03,400
Dear Miss Cooper, I'm writing to confirm the final quotation for the full-page back cover color advertisement you requested for the spring issue of Viola Magazine.

133
00:08:04,490 --> 00:08:05,350
That's great.

134
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:17,320
The final costing, including advert design and production, comes to 45,600 RMB.

135
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:24,070
We want payment 10 working days before publication, or we will cancel the ad.

136
00:08:25,010 --> 00:08:25,880
Thanks for...

137
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:29,560
Whoa, okay, back up a second Tracy.

138
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:30,270
That's too direct.

139
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:36,910
Can I suggest you say, please note that the final payment is due two working weeks before publication.

140
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:39,040
You don't want to offend her.

141
00:08:39,340 --> 00:08:40,980
Oops, okay, you're right.

142
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:44,220
Then I can just end with, all the best, Tracy.

143
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:50,440
Maybe I'd play it safe and just finish with, yours sincerely.

144
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:51,080
That's more professional.

145
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:53,680
Oh, Melanie, you're a lifesaver.

146
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:54,500
Thank you.

147
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:04,000
So today in Fluency Builder we have a number of phrases you can use when you're writing an email.

148
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:06,800
Some of them are very polite and formal.

149
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:09,220
Some of them are very informal or casual.

150
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:13,340
Now this first phrase, Marco, is very, very formal, right?

151
00:09:14,040 --> 00:09:14,180
That's right.

152
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:18,040
You start the letter by stating, to whom it may concern.

153
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:20,660
All right, to whom it may concern.

154
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:22,880
Remember that M there.

155
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:26,440
This is a very formal phrase and we're going to take it as a phrase.

156
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:27,980
This is how you begin a formal letter.

157
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:29,560
That's right.

158
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:35,620
Usually you start this letter if it's directed to maybe a company or someone not specific.

159
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:42,160
You don't know that person's name and you're just saying, you know, whoever's reading this letter, whoever it concerns.

160
00:09:42,860 --> 00:09:43,140
That's right.

161
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:49,260
Well, I could say, dear Marco, but in this case, maybe I don't know who the name of the person I am writing this email to.

162
00:09:49,580 --> 00:09:50,420
It's a big company.

163
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:51,880
There are many people.

164
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:58,320
So if you don't know who you're writing to in a company or in an organization, you say, to whom it may concern.

165
00:09:58,880 --> 00:09:59,140
That's right.

166
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:00,880
Okay.

167
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:01,640
Now we started very formal.

168
00:10:01,980 --> 00:10:04,000
Melanie said, I think it's a little bit too formal.

169
00:10:04,080 --> 00:10:05,500
Maybe we should be more relaxed.

170
00:10:06,460 --> 00:10:08,060
So Tracy says, okay, got it.

171
00:10:08,260 --> 00:10:09,920
So how about, hey Sally, what's up?

172
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:10,820
What's up?

173
00:10:11,100 --> 00:10:11,460
Okay.

174
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:17,000
So what's up is the kind of thing I say with my friends, you know, hey Marco, what's up?

175
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:18,000
Right.

176
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:26,250
But I would never say this in a formal situation with a boss or with a, you know, an employer or with a, um, in more serious situation.

177
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:26,890
Exactly.

178
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:30,670
Especially if you're writing a, an email to a client, you don't really want to say what's up.

179
00:10:31,560 --> 00:10:32,560
No, never.

180
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:35,350
So remember this is spoken English with your friends, but not something you would write.

181
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:40,120
Um, so the key here is that you have to show some respect.

182
00:10:40,590 --> 00:10:40,870
Okay.

183
00:10:41,120 --> 00:10:44,320
So that's another great phrase, you show some respect.

184
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:45,140
All right.

185
00:10:45,320 --> 00:10:48,960
So the key here is that this is, um, the verb is to show, right?

186
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:50,280
Not to give.

187
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:52,080
Um, you want to show respect to someone.

188
00:10:52,080 --> 00:10:53,020
Well, how do you show respect?

189
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:55,420
Well, the way that you act, your behaviors.

190
00:10:55,820 --> 00:11:02,500
So if I'm polite and I say, you know, Mr. and Miss and thank you and please, these are ways to show respect.

191
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:03,320
That's right.

192
00:11:04,040 --> 00:11:05,040
That's right.

193
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:07,200
So that's the way that you are being respectful.

194
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:10,040
You are showing respect by being very polite.

195
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:19,160
For example, in Japan, it is common to take your shoes off when you go to a friend's home, their house in order to show respect to them.

196
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:19,800
That's right.

197
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:21,600
Very good.

198
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:29,340
Now, as we continue writing the letter towards the end, we wanted to play it safe and just write your sincerely to close the letter off.

199
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:30,290
All right.

200
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:31,770
So this phrase, play it safe.

201
00:11:32,240 --> 00:11:32,730
What does this mean?

202
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:37,990
When we're talking about making a decision or taking a risk, we want to play it safe.

203
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:41,790
We want to use the less risky option.

204
00:11:42,410 --> 00:11:42,730
All right.

205
00:11:42,730 --> 00:11:44,730
So the key here is that the verb is to play.

206
00:11:45,250 --> 00:11:46,170
Play it safe.

207
00:11:46,840 --> 00:11:53,000
This means that, yeah, there's maybe a risky option and a safe option and it's better to take the safe option.

208
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:54,000
Play it safe.

209
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:55,000
That's right.

210
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,000
That's right.

211
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:04,500
So that's the way that you would use this phrase to play it safe, to make a decision that doesn't have too much risk or that you know that will work very well.

212
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:09,440
And to close things off, another option to end the letter, we could say, all the best.

213
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:10,130
All the best, Tracy.

214
00:12:10,920 --> 00:12:11,920
All the best, Tracy.

215
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:12,920
All right.

216
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:14,800
So this is a way to end a letter.

217
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:24,560
You can say sincerely or best, but it's a very nice way to end if you just say all the best and then you say, you know, after that you have a comma and then you sign your name.

218
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:25,220
That's right.

219
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:27,000
All right.

220
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,620
Let's listen to our dialogue one last time and we'll be back to talk a little bit more.

